window.docContents[239].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.AEAD.WireAeadDefs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___AEAD___WireAeadDefs":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/AEAD › WireAeadDefs.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 139 of 203 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (14)structure WireAEADabbrev wireSpecabbrev WireAdversaryabbrev WireStatedef idealEncdef wireEncryptdef wireDecryptdef wireOtccaGamedef wireOtccaAdvantagestructure WireCodecdef WireCodec.toWireEncdef wireEncodedef libsignalReceiveWiredef libsignalWireAead\n\nWire OT-CCA game and libsignal's per-message AEAD on raw wire bytesTrusted in part. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use only 13 definitions of this file, listed in Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md §3: read them. Everything else here is checked by Lean.Because libsignal authenticates the raw received bytes ids ‖ ser while parsing ser with a non-canonical protobuf parser, ciphertexts in WireAEAD are raw (wire bytes, tag) pairs rather than structured (header, body, tag) tuples. This file defines the wire OT-CCA game (wireSpec, WireState, idealEnc, wireEncrypt, wireDecrypt, wireOtccaGame, wireOtccaAdvantage) and libsignal's concrete instance libsignalWireAead (WireCodec, wireEncode, libsignalReceiveWire; DEV-AEAD2, DEV-AEAD4, DEV-AEAD7, DEV-LS30).\n\n\n\nstructure WireAEADL35–51 ↗#Deterministic AEAD on raw wire bytes (w, τ), with key K, associated data AD, public header H serialized into w and recovered by dec, sender plaintext M, receiver plaintext M', ideal-world body C, and tag T.\n\n\n\nabbrev wireSpecL57–61 ↗#The wire OT-CCA oracles: a one-time encrypt(a, h, m) and an unbounded decrypt(a, (w, τ)) on arbitrary wire bytes.\n\nabbrev WireAdversaryL63–65 ↗#A wire OT-CCA adversary, with its own coins (unifSpec) and the wire oracles.\n\nabbrev WireStateL67–69 ↗#Game state: key, hidden bit, and the challenge (a*, (w*, τ*)) (none before encrypt). This is OTCCAState of CryptoGames/Primitives.lean with ciphertext List (BitVec 8) × T.\n\ndef idealEncL71–76 ↗#The ideal ciphertext for header h: (encode h c, τ) with uniform body c and tag τ.\n\ndef wireEncryptL78–86 ↗#encrypt(a, h, m): one-time; b = false real, b = true the ideal ciphertext for h.\n\ndef wireDecryptL88–96 ↗#decrypt(a, (w, τ)) on any bytes: rejects the exact challenge (a*, (w*, τ*)); always rejects when b = true. A different w that parses to the challenge's header and body is not the challenge (this is what a raw-bytes MAC must reject).\n\ndef wireOtccaGameL98–113 ↗#Wire OT-CCA hidden-bit game.DEV-AEAD10 (libsignal): OT-CCA on raw wire bytes. ACD19 Fig. 1 decrypts structured ciphertexts and its ideal ciphertext is uniform. libsignal MACs the raw received bytes and parses them non-canonically, so here decryption takes arbitrary bytes, and the ideal ciphertext is encode h of a uniform body with a uniform tag (the header stays public). Effect: none; security is proved for every codec.\n\ndef wireOtccaAdvantageL115–119 ↗#Adv^{wire-ot-cca}(A), the bias 2·|Pr[b = b'] − 1/2|.\n\n\n\nstructure WireCodecL131–136 ↗#Abstract wire codec for a SignalMessage header H and arbitrary-length byte body.\n\ndef WireCodec.toWireEncL138–147 ↗#Restrict a byte-body WireCodec to the fixed-block WireEnc H n on 16·n-byte bodies.\n\ndef wireEncodeL149–152 ↗#Sender wire bytes for header h and CBC ciphertext blocks c.\n\ndef libsignalReceiveWireL154–204 ↗#Receiver decryption on arbitrary wire bytes (ser, tag) under key k = ((cipher_key, iv), mac_key): verify HMAC-SHA256/8 over ids ‖ ser, parse ser, CBC-decrypt and unpad the body via cbcDecRaw, and check addressCheckPasses (addrs h) expected.DEV-AEAD11 (libsignal): only the address-checking path. Only the local_address = Some path is modeled (verify_mac_with_addresses, triple_ratchet.rs:257–263). The legacy path that calls plain verify_mac when local_address = None (session_cipher_legacy.rs:693–705) is not. Effect: out of scope (the legacy path).DEV-AEAD12 (libsignal): key selection is not part of the AEAD. libsignal selects the message keys from the unauthenticated header before verify_mac (triple_ratchet.rs:225–254). Here the key k is given: key selection belongs to the FS-AEAD (forward-secure AEAD) layer. Effect: none.\n\ndef libsignalWireAeadL179–200 ↗#libsignal's per-message WireAEAD: AES-256-CBC-PKCS#7 encryption (cbcPkcs7EncryptOnly E ℓ), wire serialization via codec, and 8-byte truncated HMAC-SHA256 over ids ‖ ser, paired with libsignalReceiveWire.DEV-AEAD3 (libsignal): libsignal's concrete AEAD. ACD19 assumes an abstract OT-CCA secure AEAD. libsignal's is Encrypt-then-MAC with AES-256-CBC (PKCS#7 padding) and HMAC-SHA256 truncated to 8 bytes. Effect: constants: with an 8-byte tag, every OT-CCA bound for it has a forgery term of at least (decryption queries)·2^-64.DEV-AEAD13 (libsignal): fixed expected addresses. The receiver's expected addresses expected and the header's addrs map are parameters of the scheme (the receiver's local session data), not adversarial input. Effect: none.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/AEAD/WireAeadDefs.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.AEAD.WireAeadDefs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___AEAD___WireAeadDefs"}});